<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kanjuruhan Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">760 CE – 850 CE (approx.) · capital Kanjuruhan (near Malang)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Hindu kingdom near modern Malang in East Java, known from a single Sanskrit inscription of 760 and the temple of Badut.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kanjuruhan is known almost entirely from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinoyo-inscription">Dinoyo inscription</a> of 760, found near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malang">Malang</a>. It tells how King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gajayana">Gajayana</a>, son of Dewasimha, built a temple to the sage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agastya">Agastya</a> and replaced an old wooden image with one of black stone. It is the oldest known kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The inscription shows a Shaivite court with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> priests, gifts of land and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, and a cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agastya">Agastya</a> brought from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candi-badut">Candi Badut</a>, one of the oldest temples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>, is thought to be the shrine it describes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>No later record names Kanjuruhan as an independent kingdom. In the 9th and 10th centuries inscriptions mention a Rakai Kanuruhan as a title within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom</a>, suggesting the area had been absorbed and its rulers became regional lords.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 760 CE – 850 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Maritime Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:74.83%;width:1.79%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>760 CE – 850 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kanjuruhan (near Malang)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dewasimha, Gajayana</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism (Shaivism)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit (inscriptions), Old Javanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kanjuruhan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=805&amp;focus=kanjuruhan-kingdom" aria-label="Kanjuruhan Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7499 2211 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kanjuruhan Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8208 2754h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7819 2467h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7819 2467h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Indonesia. <a href="/atlas?year=805&amp;focus=kanjuruhan-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
